# E6-D1-FOUNDATION campaign scope selector for .github/workflows/d1-merge-train.yml
# on the docs/replatform-program integration branch. Bump this file to re-trigger
# the live D1 lane (it is on the workflow's push path filter).
#   bounded    = E6F-03 networked smoke + E6F-05 topology network-denial (fast)
#   foundation = the full E6F-01..07 gate campaign (bounded + tests/d1/e6f-*.test.mjs)
AOA_D1_CAMPAIGN=foundation
# Re-trigger nonce: CLI-006 the execution-ownership seam. Tasks 2b/3/4/6 all land in
# server/src ONLY — the suppression seam at the adapter call site, the projector on the
# JOB-005 ingest hook, cancel routing across the five writers, and the outer-finally
# catch — and server/src is NOT on this lane's push path filter, so without this bump
# the live 2-replica D1 lane silently would not rebuild the control-plane image or
# exercise any of it. This bit DEP-009 already.
AOA_D1_RETRIGGER=mig-005-006-007-lanes-cd
# Second bump, and the reason is the trap itself: the FIRST bump proved the lane on
# 8324e434b, but the adversarial-review fixes that followed (H1 cancel-outcome
# routing, H2 guarded marker write, H3 issue-lock narrowing, M6 wake predicate,
# M4/M5 projector-loop hoist) are ALL server/src-only and therefore invisible to
# this lane's push path filter. A green D1 on the earlier SHA says nothing about
# the code that actually ships. Re-prove the live 2-replica topology on the final
# state.
#
# THIRD bump, same reason again: round 2 of the fix review (the fifth cancel
# writer honouring writeLegacyTerminal, and a fence revoke on marker-write
# failure) is once more server/src-only. The rule this keeps proving: bump AFTER
# the last server/src change, not once per ticket.

# DSK-001 Lane B / B6. The device-credential gate lands in packages/db (the fenced
# secret-resolve decision + its transaction) and server/src (the fixture bridge and the
# integration suites). NEITHER is on this lane's push path filter -- it covers
# docker/**, tests/d1/** and the compose/lib scripts -- so without this bump the live
# 2-replica lane would not rebuild the control-plane image and would exercise none of
# it. The same trap bit CLI-006 three times and DEP-009 once.

# DSK-001 Lanes C and D. The previous bump proved Lane B on dc4b57f6f; 10 files and ~987
# insertions across server/src and packages/db have landed since -- neither is on this
# lane's push path filter, so a green D1 on the earlier SHA says nothing about the code
# that now ships. Two changes in particular need the LIVE topology rather than a unit
# suite:
#
#   C1  executionTargetToAdapterConfig now THROWS for an unmapped target kind instead of
#       returning null. The D1 stack routes real work to real targets; if any of them is a
#       kind the exhaustive switch does not name, the run fails rather than degrading -- and
#       that is precisely the behaviour change worth proving against a live stack.
#   D2  desktopDeviceRoutes is dynamically imported INSIDE the distributed-execution flag
#       block, and the D1 control plane boots with that flag ON. A failed import there is a
#       BOOT failure on both replicas, which no unit test can observe.
#
# Bumped AFTER the last server/src change, per the trap that bit CLI-006 three times.

# DSK-002 Lane A. `server/src/services/folder-grant.ts` gains a REQUIRED `presented`
# parameter and a new `folder-grant-binding.ts` module; `packages/worker-daemon` gains the
# capture-root guard. Neither path is on this lane's push path filter (docker/**,
# tests/d1/** and the compose/lib scripts), so a green D1 on the previous SHA says nothing
# about what now ships.
#
# HONESTY NOTE ON THE RISK, because the bump is cheap and a wrong judgement is not: this
# change is INERT in production. `resolveCapturedPath` and `admitCapture` still have zero
# production callers, so no live request path can reach the new binding and a boot failure
# is not reachable through it. The bump is here because the rule is "touches server/src",
# and every previous attempt to reason a change was too small to matter is exactly how
# CLI-006 got bitten three times and DEP-009 once. The lane is cheaper than being wrong.

# DSK-002 Lane D. The previous bump proved Lane A on 1bd85461a (D1 Merge Train green).
# Since then `server/src` gained the device-local activation policy and a corrected
# deferral comment on the broker port. Same trap, same rule: bump AFTER the last
# server/src change, not once per ticket. Lanes B and C are worker-daemon-only and are
# not what this bump is for.

# REL-004 Lane C (clause 3a). The kill switch is now WIRED into the worker poll, and the
# change is entirely `server/src` + `packages/worker-daemon` + `packages/db` -- none of which
# is on this lane's push path filter. Same rule, bumped AFTER the last server/src change.
#
# THIS ONE IS NOT INERT, unlike the DSK-002 Lane A bump above, and that is the reason to be
# careful rather than cheap about it. Two changes reach a live boot and a live request path:
#
#   1  Migration 0261 grants `aoa_app` SELECT on `instance_settings`, and the manifest entries
#      that must match it land in the same commit. `assertExactServingRoleAuthority` compares
#      the LIVE role's `has_table_privilege` against `appTablePrivileges()` at startup, so a
#      mismatch is `distributed_execution_app_authority` and BOTH replicas refuse to boot.
#      No unit test can observe that; only a live stack can. (It already bit locally: a stale
#      `packages/db/dist/migrations` made the manifest expect a grant the database did not
#      have, and the real server subprocess crashed exactly this way.)
#
#   2  Every poll now performs one extra read before the lease transaction opens, and can
#      answer `drain` instead of `offer`/`no_work`. The two-replica lane is the only place
#      that exercises the read under real concurrency against the real serving role.

# SECOND bump, and the reason is the trap this file keeps documenting. The first bump proved the
# lane on 4d5bcbead and it was GREEN — but `verify` then failed, and the fix moved the grant out
# of `JOB_CONTROL_LEGACY_GRANTS` into its own `KILL_SWITCH_POLICY_APP_GRANTS`, changing BOTH
# `job-control-legacy-grants.ts` and `distributed-execution-databases.ts`. Those are exactly the
# constants `assertExactServingRoleAuthority` compares against the live role at startup, and both
# are `server/src`-only — invisible to this lane's push path filter. A green D1 on the earlier SHA
# says nothing about the manifest that now ships. Bump AFTER the last server/src change.

# REL-004 Lane D. Three `server/src` runtime changes the D1 lane must exercise, none of them on
# its push path filter: (1) `scheduleWarmSandboxReaper` moved OUT of the
# `config.heartbeatSchedulerEnabled` block to module scope, so it now registers on every boot —
# a registration change is exactly the class only a live bring-up can falsify; (2) the sweep gained
# two new arms and two new `environment_leases` queries; (3) `execution-kill-switch-policy.ts`
# gained an always-loaded consumer, so its module graph is no longer dormant. Bumped AFTER the last
# server/src change, per the rule this file keeps re-proving.

# SECOND Lane D bump, and the trap caught me again in the same session. The previous bump proved
# the reaper's registration move and its new arms — but TWO more `server/src` runtime changes
# landed afterwards and would otherwise never reach the live lane:
#
#   1. `environment-runtime.ts` now stamps `metadata.keyGeneration` on the ACQUIRE path. That is a
#      live sandbox-creation path and the riskiest change in the lane; a bookkeeping lookup that
#      failed an acquire would be a real outage, which is precisely what a live bring-up tests.
#   2. `claimTerminalUncleaned`'s predicate changed from `IS DISTINCT FROM 'success'` to
#      `{NULL,'pending'}` — the compare-and-swap fix. A CAS is exactly the thing a two-replica
#      topology falsifies and a single-process test cannot.
#
# The rule this file keeps re-proving: bump AFTER the last server/src change, not once per ticket.

# MIG-005/006/007 Lane A + the Lane B read-only foundation. Two live-path reasons to
# spend the lane rather than one bookkeeping one:
#
#   1. `job-submission.ts` now calls the SHARED `submitJobSourceIdentity` /
#      `submitJobSourceWorkloadType` instead of its own private copies. Behaviour-
#      preserving by inspection, but every real submission on the D1 journey runs
#      through both, and "behaviour-preserving by inspection" is what a live lane is for.
#   2. `with-tenant-tx.ts` / `tenant-context.ts` gained a read-only sibling next to the
#      wrapper that every distributed submission and lease already uses. The addition has
#      no runtime caller yet, but it sits in the file that does.
#
# The comparator + heartbeat seam changes are confined to the default-off shadow branch
# and would not, alone, justify the 45 minutes.

# Lanes C+D. Three LIVE user-visible paths in `server/src` gained a call that did not
# exist before: `cli-mode.ts` (every Commander turn), `aoa-agents/runner.ts` (every crew
# dispatch) and `one-shot-sandbox-cli.ts` (every extraction, compaction and readiness
# probe). Each is guarded, deadline-bounded and inert with the port unregistered — which
# is exactly the claim a live two-replica bring-up is for. `index.ts` also now registers
# the port inside the distributed-execution composition, so the boot path changed.
#
# Bump AFTER the last server/src change, not once per ticket.
